# TLS + routing for this instance. Both hostnames come from the environment ({$FORGE_DOMAIN},
# {$FORGE_ADMIN_DOMAIN}), so nothing here names your domain and this file needs no edit to move.
#
# Caddy provisions a Let's Encrypt certificate for each host automatically — which means the DNS records must
# already point at this box when you first bring the stack up, and ports 80 and 443 must be reachable.
#
# The root domain serves TWO fronts, split by path. Most of it is the STOREFRONT — the vitrine, the half you
# are free to fork. `/checkout`, `/account`, `/.well-known/` and the checkout's own assets go to the CHECKOUT
# container: the part that handles money and sign-in, that we keep evolving, and that you do not have to
# maintain to own your storefront. Same hostname on purpose — a subdomain would put the cart and the session
# cookies on a different origin, and a shopper would lose their cart on the way to paying.
#
# The browser never reaches /v1/*: the fronts and the admin talk to the kernel server-side over the compose
# network. What IS public are the doors meant for callers that are not browsers — an ERP with an API key, an
# agent over MCP, the CLI:
#   /v1/*                — the port: public reads, the command face, the internal read face (the kernel
#                          authenticates every request; an anonymous call to an authenticated face is refused
#                          by the kernel, not by this file)
#   /webhooks/payment/*  — the payment provider POSTs here
#   /webhooks/channel/*  — a channel provider POSTs delivery receipts here (the address a channel app's
#                          ficha prints for you to paste into the provider's console)
#   /health              — liveness, for your monitoring
#
# And ONE door that is deliberately shut: /v1/control/*, the platform face. It carries cross-tenant power, so
# it answers 403 from the public interface unless you name a source range for it. The full map, and what to do
# if you want it reachable from your VPN, is in docs/conventions/surface-exposure.md.
#
# ⚠️ Coarse per-IP protection (an abuse cap in front of everything) is NOT in this file: the stock `caddy:2`
# image has no rate-limit module, and a directive this binary does not know would not degrade one route — it
# would stop the whole file loading, and your STORE with it. The per-credential cap is inside the kernel and
# is always on. The recipe for the edge layer, if you want it, is in the doc above.

{$FORGE_DOMAIN} {
	# Certificates you were GIVEN, rather than ones Caddy issues. If a CDN sits in front of this box in
	# full-strict mode, it terminates TLS and the ACME challenge can never reach here — so automatic
	# renewal cannot work and the certificate expires ~90 days after it was issued. The way out is the
	# CDN's Origin Certificate: `cat origin.crt origin.key > caddy/certs/origin.pem` and restart.
	#
	# An EMPTY folder is the normal state and changes nothing — Caddy keeps issuing its own certificates.
	# One declaration covers BOTH hostnames: the certificate pool is process-wide, not per-site.
	tls {
		load /etc/caddy/certs
	}
	# Who may reach the control face. Empty is not something `remote_ip` accepts, so "nobody" is written as
	# loopback — a source address no request arriving over the internet can have. Put your VPN / bastion CIDRs
	# in FORGE_CONTROL_ALLOW_CIDR (space-separated) if you want it reachable, and leave it alone otherwise.
	@control_allowed remote_ip {$FORGE_CONTROL_ALLOW_CIDR:127.0.0.1/32}
	handle /v1/control/* {
		route {
			reverse_proxy @control_allowed kernel:3000
			respond "the control face is not exposed on this interface" 403
		}
	}
	handle /v1/* {
		reverse_proxy kernel:3000
	}
	# ★ SURFACES-EXT S2 — THE REMOTE MCP ENDPOINT, AND IT IS ITS OWN PATH ON PURPOSE. MCP negotiates its
	# version in-band (the `MCP-Protocol-Version` header), so the route carries none — which is exactly why it
	# does not live under /v1/* and has to be named here. It was NOT, and that is the defect this block closes:
	# S2 mounted it in the kernel, S3 drew the edge, and the seam between the two slices had no owner. The
	# comment at the top of this file already promised "an MCP agent" among the callers reaching the kernel,
	# so the file was asserting a route it did not carry. Measured on Staging 2026-08-24: POST /mcp answered
	# 405 from the STOREFRONT (Next refusing a method on a page that is not there), never reaching the kernel,
	# while /health and /v1/read/internal/* both landed correctly. `infra/cicd/control-exposure-selftest.sh`
	# now lists /mcp among the faces that MUST answer, so an edge that forgets it again fails the build.
	handle /mcp {
		reverse_proxy kernel:3000
	}
	handle /webhooks/payment/* {
		reverse_proxy kernel:3000
	}
	# ★★ QA17/FM3 — THE CHANNEL CALLBACK SEAM, and it was missing while the admin was already telling
	# merchants to paste its address into their provider's console. The provider POSTs delivery receipts here
	# (apps/api/src/channel-webhook-adapter.ts, keyed on the INSTALLATION so two merchants on one box hold two
	# different addresses). Without this block the request fell through to the storefront and came back as its
	# 404 — an integration that can only fail, from an address a screen handed out.
	handle /webhooks/channel/* {
		reverse_proxy kernel:3000
	}
	handle /health {
		reverse_proxy kernel:3000
	}
	# ★★ CHECKOUT-APP (C3) — THE SECOND FRONT ON THIS HOST, AND THE ONLY THING THAT PUTS IT THERE.
	#
	# `apps/checkout` is a deployable of its own (checkout, account, login, orders); `apps/storefront` is the
	# vitrine a customer forks. They share ONE hostname and split by PATH — never by subdomain, because a
	# subdomain would put `forge_cart` and `forge_customer_session` on another origin and the shopper would
	# lose the cart on the way to paying for it. These five blocks ARE that split; without them the checkout
	# container is running and unreachable, and every address below answers the vitrine's 404.
	#
	# Declared in packages/codegen/src/control-exposure.ts (EDGE_HANDLES) and compared against this file in
	# BOTH directions by infra/cicd/control-exposure-selftest.sh — a handle here that the declaration has not
	# heard of is red, and so is one there that this file does not carry.
	#
	# ⚠️ ORDER IS NOT WHAT DECIDES THIS. Caddy matches `handle` blocks by how SPECIFIC their path is, not by
	# where they appear, so these win over the bare fall-through below wherever they are written. They are
	# written here because a reader needs to see them before the fall-through, not because Caddy does.
	handle /checkout* {
		reverse_proxy checkout:3000
	}
	handle /account* {
		reverse_proxy checkout:3000
	}
	# ⚠️ `/api/account/orders/*` AND NOT `/api/account*`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A MEASURED ONE.
	# `/api/account/status` is STILL the vitrine's route (apps/storefront/src/app/api/account/status/route.ts)
	# — the header asks it after hydration to choose between "Entrar" and the shopper's name, and reading a
	# session is exactly what the vitrine is allowed to do. Widen this to the whole prefix and that fetch
	# lands on a deployable that does not serve it: the header is permanently signed out, the 404 happens
	# where no human is looking, and the account page itself still works, so nobody reports it. The selftest
	# probes `/api/account/status` as a STOREFRONT path for this reason.
	handle /api/account/orders/* {
		reverse_proxy checkout:3000
	}
	# RFC 8615: the whole prefix is host-level, never per-store. It is the CHECKOUT's because the social login
	# is — Apple fetches `apple-developer-domain-association.txt` at the bare host before it will trust the
	# Services ID whose handshake starts at `/account/social/<provider>/start`.
	handle /.well-known/* {
		reverse_proxy checkout:3000
	}
	# ★★ THE ASSETS, AND THIS IS THE ONE THAT FAILS IN SILENCE. Two Next servers on one origin both ask the
	# browser for `/_next/static/…`; whichever container wins that namespace serves chunks the other build has
	# never heard of. `assetPrefix: '/_checkout'` (apps/checkout/next.config.mjs) moves this app's assets to
	# their own prefix, and this block is the half at the edge. Without it the shopper meets an UNSTYLED
	# checkout holding their card number: the HTML arrives, no server logs anything, and the only trace is a
	# 404 for a stylesheet nobody was watching.
	#
	# ⚠️⚠️ `handle_path`, NOT `handle`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS SIX CHARACTERS AND THE WHOLE THING. `handle_path`
	# STRIPS the matched prefix before proxying. MEASURED on the real image (C3): a Next server with
	# `assetPrefix: '/_checkout'` emits the prefixed URL into its HTML and then answers **404** to it — it
	# serves those files at `/_next/static/…` and nothing else. The prefix is an instruction to THIS FILE, not
	# a route the app owns. With a bare `handle` every chunk reaches the right container and comes back 404,
	# which is the same unstyled checkout the prefix exists to prevent, one layer further in.
	handle_path /_checkout/* {
		reverse_proxy checkout:3000
	}
	# ★★ THE GENERATED ROUTES OF THE APPS WHOSE SLOTS LIVE HERE (slice C2, spec §2.3b). `forge.wiring.publicRoutes`
	# lets an app declare a public URL, and the composition writes it into the consumer where that app's SLOTS
	# are: `reviews` draws "Avaliar produtos" in the `order.*` slots, so `/apps/reviews/submit` is generated
	# into apps/checkout — and it asks for account-class injections, which is why it may not live in the
	# deployable a customer forks. Without this block the form POSTs to the vitrine, which does not serve it:
	# a shopper writes a review of something they bought and gets a 404 on submit.
	#
	# ⚠️ WHOLE-PREFIX, AND WHAT MAKES THAT SAFE IS A FACT ABOUT TODAY'S COMPOSITION. The one generated app
	# route that stays with the vitrine is the feed's, and it is `/feeds/google.xml` — outside this prefix (the
	# selftest probes it as a STOREFRONT path for exactly that reason). An app whose slots live in the vitrine
	# and that declared a route under `/apps/` would be silently taken by this block.
	handle /apps/* {
		reverse_proxy checkout:3000
	}
	# ★ THE CHECKOUT'S COMPONENT GALLERY (slice C4), the sibling of the vitrine's `/ui-storefront`. That one
	# needs no block because the vitrine is the fall-through; this one is on the other side of the split.
	handle /ui-checkout* {
		reverse_proxy checkout:3000
	}
	handle {
		reverse_proxy storefront:3000
	}
}

# The admin on its own hostname, with its own DNS record and its own certificate.
{$FORGE_ADMIN_DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy admin:3000
}

# Hostnames beyond those two — the case being a SECOND ADMIN, for a second tenant this instance holds. They
# go in caddy/extra/, one file each, instead of here: this file needs no edit to move between boxes, and an
# empty folder is the normal state. Caddy accepts a pattern that matches nothing (one `warn` line in the log
# and it carries on), so leaving the folder empty changes nothing at all.
#
# A hostname added there behaves like the two above — Caddy matches site blocks by how specific they are,
# not by where they appear — and gets its certificate automatically. See caddy/extra/README.md for the whole
# recipe (the container, the hostname, the reload).
import /etc/caddy/extra/*.caddy
